I fixed the doctor footer and committed it as 1cacda26 (Fixes #1074). Build, clippy, fmt and the full test suite pass.

What was wrong: I checked every row doctor prints, and /fix can't repair any of them. /fix re-runs your project's build, lint and test commands, and none of doctor's rows (version, git, API key, config, context, curl, memory dir, RTK, toolchain presence, skill cost) is one of those. So the footer should never point at /fix.

Before and after in a fresh git init dir, running yoyo doctor from the shell:
```
before:  → 3 issues found. Try /fix to attempt repairs, or /health for a full check.
after:   → 3 warnings. See each row above for details.
```

• Counts: failures and warnings are now counted separately, e.g. → 2 checks failed, 1 warning. …. A run where everything passes still prints no footer.
• Shell users: the footer no longer names any command, so shell users can't be sent to a slash command. That made a REPL/shell switch unnecessary, so I left dispatch_sub.rs unchanged. I didn't add an unused parameter just because the task suggested a surface flag.
• Wording: "See each row above for details" is softer than the task's suggested "each row above says what to do", because that would be false: the Config file row reports what's missing without saying what to do.
• Tests:
  • A table of real row sets compared against the full expected string, with all-pass as the no-change case.
  • A sweep of every fail/warn count from 0 to 3 checking that no command name appears in any footer.
  • Two old tests asserted the /fix pointer, which was the bug itself. I rewrote them to pin the new wording rather than deleting them.
  • I put the old wording back, ran the tests and restored the file in a single command: 5 tests went red, including the table row for the fresh-dir case. After restoring, all 7 passed and the NEUTERED marker was gone.
• Docs: docs/src/usage/commands.md described the old /fix pointer, so I updated it.

Not fixed here (filed as #1075): two rows' own advice tells shell users to run REPL commands. "run /init" has a shell equivalent (yoyo init). "run /remember to create" doesn't: yoyo remember x isn't a subcommand and falls through to prompt mode. A bare /remember in the REPL only prints usage and doesn't create the directory either.

I posted the before/after on #1074, since the push will close it.
